    "kernel_code": "1: \"\"\"Standalone RMSNorm backward kernels adapted from Liger Kernel.\n2: \n3: Copyright 2024 LinkedIn Corporation. See LICENSE for license and attribution.\n4: 2026 adaptation: standalone CUDA interface, FP16 activations and FP32 weight\n5: accumulation buffer. The public contract defines the supported casting policy.\n6: \"\"\"\n7: import torch\n8: import triton\n9: import triton.language as tl\n10: \n11: \n12: @triton.jit\n13: def _row_backward(DY, DX, X, W, RSTD, PARTIAL, M: tl.constexpr,\n14:                   N: tl.constexpr, ROWS: tl.constexpr, B: tl.constexpr):\n15:     pid = tl.program_id(0).to(tl.int64)\n16:     cols = tl.arange(0, B)\n17:     mask = cols < N\n18:     weight = tl.load(W + cols, mask=mask, other=0.0)\n19:     dw = tl.zeros((B,), tl.float32)\n20:     start = pid * ROWS\n21:     stop = tl.minimum(start + ROWS, M)\n22:     for row in range(start, stop):\n23:         dy = tl.load(DY + row * N + cols, mask=mask, other=0.0)\n24:         x = tl.load(X + row * N + cols, mask=mask, other=0.0).to(tl.float32)\n25:         r = tl.load(RSTD + row)\n26:         m = (dy * weight).to(tl.float32)\n27:         dx = r * m\n28:         dx += r * (-(1.0 / N) * r * r * tl.sum(m * x, 0) * x)\n29:         dw += dy * (x * r).to(tl.float16)\n30:         tl.store(DX + row * N + cols, dx, mask=mask)\n31:     tl.store(PARTIAL + pid * N + cols, dw, mask=mask)\n32: \n33: \n34: @triton.jit\n35: def _block_backward(DY, DX, X, W, RSTD, PARTIAL, M: tl.constexpr,\n36:                     N: tl.constexpr, B: tl.constexpr, BR: tl.constexpr):\n37:     pid = tl.program_id(0).to(tl.int64)\n38:     programs = tl.num_programs(0)\n39:     cols = tl.arange(0, B)\n40:     col_mask = cols < N\n41:     weight = tl.load(W + cols, mask=col_mask, other=0.0)\n42:     dw = tl.zeros((B,), tl.float32)\n43:     for start in range(pid * BR, M, programs * BR):\n44:         rows = start + tl.arange(0, BR)\n45:         mask = (rows[:, None] < M) & col_mask[None, :]\n46:         dy = tl.load(DY + rows[:, None] * N + cols[None, :], mask=mask, other=0.0)\n47:         x = tl.load(X + rows[:, None] * N + cols[None, :], mask=mask, other=0.0).to(tl.float32)\n48:         r = tl.load(RSTD + rows, mask=rows < M, other=0.0)\n49:         m = (dy * weight[None, :]).to(tl.float32)\n50:         dx = r[:, None] * m\n51:         dx += r[:, None] * (\n52:             -(1.0 / N) * (r * r * tl.sum(m * x, 1))[:, None] * x\n53:         )\n54:         dw += tl.sum((dy * (x * r[:, None]).to(tl.float16)).to(tl.float32), 0)\n55:         tl.store(DX + rows[:, None] * N + cols[None, :], dx, mask=mask)\n56:     tl.store(PARTIAL + pid * N + cols, dw, mask=col_mask)\n57: \n58: \n59: def run(x, weight, dy, rstd):\n60:     \"\"\"Return (dx: FP16[M,N], dw: FP32[N]); preserve all inputs.\"\"\"\n61:     x, weight, dy, rstd = (t.resolve_neg() for t in (x, weight, dy, rstd))\n62:     m, n = x.shape\n63:     assert x.is_cuda and x.is_contiguous() and dy.is_contiguous()\n64:     assert weight.is_contiguous() and rstd.is_contiguous()\n65:     assert x.dtype == dy.dtype == weight.dtype == torch.float16\n66:     assert rstd.dtype == torch.float32\n67:     assert dy.shape == x.shape and weight.shape == (n,) and rstd.shape == (m,)\n68:     assert x.device == weight.device == dy.device == rstd.device\n69:     assert 1 <= m <= 4096 and 16 <= n <= 512\n70:     block = triton.next_power_of_2(n)\n71:     programs = min(torch.cuda.get_device_properties(x.device).multi_processor_count, m)\n72:     partial = torch.empty((programs, n), dtype=torch.float32, device=x.device)\n73:     dx = torch.empty_like(dy)\n74:     if block > 256 or m < 512:\n75:         _row_backward[(programs,)](\n76:             dy, dx, x, weight, rstd, partial, m, n,\n77:             ROWS=triton.cdiv(m, programs), B=block,\n78:             num_warps=4, enable_fp_fusion=False,\n79:         )\n80:     else:\n81:         _block_backward[(programs,)](\n82:             dy, dx, x, weight, rstd, partial, m, n,\n83:             B=block, BR=16, num_warps=4, enable_fp_fusion=False,\n84:         )\n85:     dw = partial.sum(dim=0)\n86:     return dx, dw\n87: \n88: \n89: def reference(x, weight, dy, rstd):\n90:     \"\"\"Independent tensor reference for the public mixed-precision contract.\n91: \n92:     FP64 evaluates the analytic dx formula and accumulates the individually\n93:     rounded weight-gradient terms. This function can also run on CPU.\n94:     \"\"\"\n95:     x64 = x.to(torch.float64)\n96:     r64 = rstd.to(torch.float64)[:, None]\n97:     m64 = (dy * weight).to(torch.float64)\n98:     dx64 = r64 * (m64 - x64 * r64.square() * (m64 * x64).mean(dim=1, keepdim=True))\n99:     h = (x.float() * rstd[:, None]).to(torch.float16)\n100:     terms64 = (dy * h).to(torch.float64)\n101:     return dx64, terms64.sum(dim=0)\n102: \n103: \n104: def error_ratios(outputs, inputs):\n105:     \"\"\"A value <= 1 for each output satisfies the numerical contract.\"\"\"\n106:     dx, dw = outputs\n107:     x, weight, dy, rstd = inputs\n108:     ref_dx, ref_dw = reference(*inputs)\n109:     h = (x.float() * rstd[:, None]).to(torch.float16)\n110:     abs_terms = (dy * h).to(torch.float64).abs().sum(dim=0)\n111:     dx_limit = 0.002 + 0.002 * ref_dx.abs()\n112:     dw_limit = 0.00001 + 0.00001 * abs_terms\n113:     return {\n114:         \"dx\": float(((dx.double() - ref_dx).abs() / dx_limit).max().item()),\n115:         \"dw\": float(((dw.double() - ref_dw).abs() / dw_limit).max().item()),\n116:     }\n117: \n118: \n119: def make_inputs(device=\"cuda\", rows=768, cols=128, seed=0):\n120:     \"\"\"An ordinary example, not a restriction of the supported input domain.\"\"\"\n121:     g = torch.Generator(device=\"cpu\").manual_seed(seed)\n122:     x = (2 * torch.rand((rows, cols), generator=g) - 1).half()\n123:     weight = (0.5 + torch.rand((cols,), generator=g)).half()\n124:     dy = (2 * torch.rand((rows, cols), generator=g) - 1).half()\n125:     rstd = torch.rsqrt(x.float().square().mean(dim=1) + 1e-5)\n126:     return tuple(t.to(device) for t in (x, weight, dy, rstd))",
    "passed": "unknown",
    "problem_text": "Verify a CUDA RMSNorm backward implementation with the stated mixed-precision\ncasting policy. The task concerns this implementation over the entire domain\nbelow, not only the example returned by make_inputs().\n\nInputs (all contiguous, non-overlapping, on the same CUDA device):\n- x and dy: FP16 tensors of shape [M, N]; weight: FP16 tensor of shape [N].\n- rstd: FP32 tensor of shape [M], the saved reciprocal RMS from the forward pass.\n- 1 <= M <= 4096; 16 <= N <= 512. Non-powers of two are included.\n- All values are finite; |x| <= 4, |dy| <= 4, |weight| <= 2.\n- The RMS of each row of x lies in [0.25, 4]. For epsilon = 1e-5,\n  rstd agrees with 1/sqrt(mean_j(x[i,j]^2) + epsilon) to relative error <= 1e-6.\n\nLet H16 denote rounding to IEEE FP16, and F32 rounding to IEEE FP32.\nAs in the Llama mixed-precision backward policy, define:\n  m[i,j] = H16(dy[i,j] * weight[j])\n  h[i,j] = H16(F32(F32(x[i,j]) * rstd[i]))\n  t[i,j] = H16(dy[i,j] * h[i,j])\nThe target outputs, with sums below evaluated in real arithmetic, are:\n  dx[i,j] = rstd[i] * (m[i,j] - x[i,j] * rstd[i]^2\n                        * sum_k(m[i,k] * x[i,k]) / N)\n  dw[j] = sum_i t[i,j]\nThe rounded per-element products are intentional parts of the casting policy;\nthey are not themselves implementation errors. The dw result is an FP32 main\ngradient buffer, as used for stable parameter-gradient accumulation.\n\nReturn a pair: dx of shape [M,N] and dtype FP16, dw of shape [N] and dtype FP32.\nAll outputs must be finite. For every element:\n  abs(dx_output - dx_target) <= 0.002 + 0.002 * abs(dx_target)\n  abs(dw_output - dw_target) <= 1e-5 + 1e-5 * sum_i abs(t[i,j])\nThe absolute-sum scale permits cancellation without demanding relative accuracy\non a near-zero gradient. These bounds apply to all legal input values and shapes.\n\nDo not modify any input. The public reference() and error_ratios() in kernel.py\nimplement these formulas independently of the Triton kernels. You may create any\nlegal test inputs; make_inputs() is only a convenient example. Run on the frozen\nNVIDIA T4 / PyTorch 2.8.0 / Triton 3.4.0 CUDA environment.\n",
